Description: Stop at a trailing '%' in the -m/-s format instead of reading past its end
 When the -m or -s format ends in '%', modfile_name() steps onto the
 terminating NUL, warns about an unknown modifier, and the loop increment then
 reads past the end of the string.  Reproduced with AddressSanitizer:
 heap-buffer-overflow read at modfile_name.c.
 .
 A trailing '%' now gets its own warning and ends the format.
Author: Ash Pera <ashley.pera@noaa.gov>
Bug-Debian: https://bugs.debian.org/1155614
Forwarded: https://bugs.debian.org/1155614
Last-Updated: 2026-11-27
--- a/modfile_name.c
+++ b/modfile_name.c
@@ -89,6 +89,10 @@ char *modfile_name (const char *modulename,
                 case '%':
                     modfile[mi++] = '%';
                     break;
+                case '\0':
+                    warning ("Trailing '%%' in modfile format '%s'", fmt);
+                    i--;  /* so the loop stops at the NUL */
+                    break;
                 default:
                     warning ("Unknown modifier '%%%c' in modfile format '%s'",
                              fmt[i], fmt);
